The main idea is much simpler if we think about what the calculator did
to each base-B digit.

Suppose the real base-B representation of X is:
d1 d2 d3 ...

where every digit satisfies:
0 <= d[i] < B

The calculator displays each digit as:
- 0...9 -> one character, for example 7 -> "7"
- 10...B-1 -> its decimal representation, for example 14 -> "14"

So the displayed string Y is actually a concatenation of decimal representation of the base-B digits.

Therefore, our problem become:
`In how many ways we can split Y into pieces, where each piece represents one valid base-B digit?`

Each valid split corresponds to exactly one X

========================================================================
B = 16
Y = "1111"

The important observation is that, in base 16, a digit can be:
0, 1, 2, ..., 9, 10, 11, ..., 15

and calculator displays:
0..9 -> "0".."9"
10 -> "10"
11 -> "11"
12 -> "12"
...
15 -> "15"

So for our string "1111", a piece can be:
"1"  -> 1   valid
"11" -> 11  valid
"111" -> 111 invalid
"1111" -> 1111 invalid

Therefore, we need to split "1111" into pieces of either length 1 or length 2.
